```javascript
function Dog(name) { this.name = name; }
Dog.prototype.bark = function () { return `${this.name} says woof`; };
const rex = new Dog("Rex");
```

Calling `new Dog("Rex")` does three things: creates a new plain object, sets that object's internal prototype link to `Dog.prototype`, and runs `Dog` with `this` bound to the new object. `rex` itself only owns `name` — `bark` lives once on `Dog.prototype` and is found via the chain on every call.

![Vertical prototype chain from an instance up through Dog.prototype and Object.prototype to null](assets/prototype-chain-vertical.svg)

`class` is syntax, not a new inheritance model — it compiles to the exact constructor-function-plus-prototype shape from the previous slide:

```javascript
class Dog {
  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }
  bark() { return `${this.name} says woof`; }
}
// is, at runtime, equivalent to:
function Dog(name) { this.name = name; }
Dog.prototype.bark = function () { return `${this.name} says woof`; };
```

`typeof Dog === "function"` is true for the `class` version too — a class **is** a function under the hood, with a special internal flag that makes it throw if called without `new`. Methods declared inside `class` are placed on `Dog.prototype`, not copied onto each instance, exactly like the manual version. This is why `class` didn't require redesigning the language: it's a readable syntax over a mechanism that already existed.

`Object.create` sets a prototype link directly, skipping constructors, `new`, and `class` entirely — the purest form of "objects linked to objects":

```javascript
const animalMethods = {
  eat() { return `${this.name} eats`; },
};
const dog = Object.create(animalMethods);
dog.name = "Rex";
dog.eat(); // "Rex eats" — found via the prototype link

Object.getPrototypeOf(dog) === animalMethods; // true
```

No constructor function ran, no `this` was implicitly bound by `new` — `dog` is just a plain object whose prototype link happens to point at `animalMethods`. This is worth knowing because some libraries (and interview questions) build inheritance this way specifically to show they understand the chain isn't inherently tied to `class` or `function` at all.

The classic prototype bug: putting a **mutable object** (not a primitive) on a prototype, then mutating it from an instance method, assuming it's per-instance.

```javascript
function Team() {}
Team.prototype.members = []; // one array, shared by every instance

const a = new Team();
const b = new Team();
a.members.push("Alice");
b.members; // ["Alice"] — b sees a's change! Same array, via the chain.
```

`a.members.push(...)` doesn't create an own property — it looks up `members` (found on the prototype), gets the **one shared array**, and mutates it in place. Nothing about `push` creates a new array or a new binding. The fix is always the same: put mutable, per-instance state in the **constructor** (`this.members = []`), not on the prototype — primitives on a prototype are harmless because assignment always shadows rather than mutates, but objects and arrays are shared references.

`class Puppy extends Dog` links `Puppy.prototype`'s own prototype to `Dog.prototype`, extending the chain by one more link — `puppy.bark()` now falls through `Puppy.prototype` (nothing there) to `Dog.prototype` (found).

```javascript
class Puppy extends Dog {
  bark() { return `${super.bark()} (but small)`; }
}
const p = new Puppy("Rex");
p.bark(); // "Rex says woof (but small)"
```

`super.bark()` explicitly looks up `bark` **starting one link further up the chain** than `this` would — it's a deliberate chain-walk that skips the current level, not magic. `super(...)` inside a subclass constructor is the equivalent for construction: it calls `Dog`'s constructor with the new instance as `this`, which is mandatory before you can use `this` at all in a derived class's constructor.
